WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.569 --> 00:00:17.726
Over the next few days, I saw little of Mr. Rochester.

2
00:00:17.727 --> 00:00:25.884
Sometimes, when he passed me in the hall or on the stairs, he barely acknowledged me, but other times he bowed and smiled.

3
00:00:25.885 --> 00:00:31.150
His moodiness didn't offend me because I knew that I was not to blame.

4
00:00:31.151 --> 00:00:38.387
One evening he had guests to dinner and sent for my portfolio, probably to show off its contents.

5
00:00:38.388 --> 00:00:43.561
When everyone had left, he requested that Adele and I come to the dining room.

6
00:00:43.562 --> 00:00:49.877
"My gift! My gift!" exclaimed Adele as soon as she spotted a box on the table.

7
00:00:49.878 --> 00:00:56.956
"Yes, there is your present at last," said the deep and rather sarcastic voice of Mr. Rochester.

8
00:00:56.957 --> 00:01:07.609
The voice came from the depths of an immense easy chair that faced away from us and toward the fire. "Open it in silence and don't bother me."

9
00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:16.747
Adele lifted the lid and pulled out gifts wrapped in silver paper. "Oh, how beautiful!" she cried in French.

10
00:01:16.748 --> 00:01:25.016
"Is Miss Eyre there?" Mr. Rochester rose from his seat to look at me since I was still standing near the door.

11
00:01:25.017 --> 00:01:33.256
"Come sit here." He pulled another chair near his own. "I'm not fond of children's chatter.

12
00:01:33.257 --> 00:01:38.088
It would be intolerable to me to pass a whole evening alone with a brat."

13
00:01:38.089 --> 00:01:43.920
Mr. Rochester soon rang for Mrs. Fairfax and asked her to sit with Adele.

14
00:01:43.921 --> 00:01:49.670
After Mrs. Fairfax was settled, he stared at the fire, and I stared at him.

15
00:01:49.671 --> 00:01:56.335
"You examine me, Miss Eyre," he said after a few minutes. "Do you think I'm handsome?"

16
00:01:56.336 --> 00:02:01.811
I should have responded with a conventionally vague and polite reply.

17
00:02:01.812 --> 00:02:05.649
Instead I said, "No, sir."

18
00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:13.163
"Ah! There is indeed something unusual about you. Now what did you mean by that?"

19
00:02:13.164 --> 00:02:25.224
"Sir, I should have said that it's not easy to give a quick answer to questions about appearance, that tastes differ, that beauty is of little consequence—or something like that."

20
00:02:25.225 --> 00:02:34.296
"You should have said no such thing. Beauty is of little consequence indeed! You are no more pretty than I am handsome."

21
00:02:34.297 --> 00:02:43.037
He studied me as he rose from his chair and stood in front of the fireplace. "I'm in the mood to talk tonight. Speak."

22
00:02:43.038 --> 00:02:44.933
"What about, sir?"

23
00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:46.614
"Whatever you like."

24
00:02:46.615 --> 00:02:55.851
I said nothing. If he expected me to talk for the mere sake of talking and showing off, he had addressed himself to the wrong person.

25
00:02:55.852 --> 00:02:58.935
"You are silent, Miss Eyre."

26
00:02:58.936 --> 00:03:05.449
He bent his head a little toward me and with a single glance seemed to dive into my eyes.

27
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:15.920
"You're also stubborn and annoyed." He paused. "I made my request in an absurd, almost insolent form. Miss Eyre, I beg your pardon.

28
00:03:15.921 --> 00:03:25.265
The fact is, I don't want to treat you like an inferior. I claim only such superiority because of our twenty years' difference in age and experience."

29
00:03:25.266 --> 00:03:30.659
He had decided to explain himself to me, so now I had to speak too.

30
00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:36.467
"I'm willing to amuse you, sir, but I don't know what topic will interest you," I said.

31
00:03:36.468 --> 00:03:43.578
"And I don't think you have a right to command me merely because you're older or because you've seen more of the world."

32
00:03:43.579 --> 00:03:49.488
"Nature meant me to be generally a good man, but you see I am not so.

33
00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:55.581
I can read that in your eyes. I'm not a villain, merely a commonplace sinner.

34
00:03:55.582 --> 00:04:00.699
But I have a right to get pleasure out of life, and I will get it, no matter what it costs."

35
00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:07.198
"Then you'll degenerate still more, sir," I said even though I had no idea what he might have done.

36
00:04:07.199 --> 00:04:11.544
He talked some more, and I still felt him to be a mystery.

37
00:04:11.545 --> 00:04:20.282
"In time I think you'll learn to act naturally with me," he said. "I find it impossible to behave conventionally with you."

38
00:04:20.283 --> 00:04:26.853
At that point the clock struck nine. "I must put Adele to bed," I said as I stood up.

39
00:04:26.854 --> 00:04:37.172
But Adele was not quite ready for bed. She had found something special in her box and now stood at the door wearing a pink silk dress.

40
00:04:37.173 --> 00:04:48.359
"Isn't my new dress beautiful?" She danced across the room and then dropped on one knee at Mr. Rochester's feet. "Isn't that how Mama used to do it?"

41
00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:55.606
"Precisely." Then he said to me, "I'll tell you her story one day, but for now good night."

42
00:04:55.607 --> 00:05:03.669
One afternoon he did indeed tell me Adele's story. While she played on the lawn with Pilot, we walked nearby.

43
00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:12.075
Mr. Rochester explained that Adele was the daughter of an opera dancer, Celine Varens, whom he had met in Paris

44
00:05:12.076 --> 00:05:16.820
He'd been in love with Celine, but she betrayed him with another man.

45
00:05:16.821 --> 00:05:23.819
"It's strange that I'm choosing to tell you this story," he mused, "and even stranger that you'd listen to it."

46
00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:30.460
He then said that Celine had claimed Adele was his child, but he'd never believed that.

47
00:05:30.461 --> 00:05:37.825
Years after he'd broken off with Celine, she abandoned Adele and ran away to Italy with another man.

48
00:05:37.826 --> 00:05:43.211
I realized then that Celine was not dead, as Adele had been led to believe!

49
00:05:43.212 --> 00:05:49.607
So Mr. Rochester brought Adele to England and asked Mrs. Fairfax to hire a governess.

50
00:05:49.608 --> 00:05:56.711
He stopped walking and turned to me. "Now that you know this, I suppose you'll be searching for another position."

51
00:05:56.712 --> 00:06:02.954
I shook my head. "She's a lonely little orphan who regards her governess as a friend."

52
00:06:02.955 --> 00:06:12.035
"So that is your attitude," he said abruptly as he looked up at the darkening sky. "It's getting late. Time to go in."

53
00:06:12.036 --> 00:06:17.367
That night I took Adele on my lap and let her talk for as long as she liked.

54
00:06:17.368 --> 00:06:22.690
When I had retired to bed, I reviewed the tale Mr. Rochester had told me

55
00:06:22.691 --> 00:06:31.492
For some weeks his conduct had been more uniform toward to me, and I sometimes felt he was more like a relative than an employer.

56
00:06:31.493 --> 00:06:44.601
And was Mr. Rochester still ugly in my eyes? No, reader. His face was now the object I most liked to see. Yet I had not forgotten his faults.

57
00:06:44.602 --> 00:06:50.743
He was proud and moody, but I believed his moodiness was due to some cruelty of fate.

58
00:06:50.744 --> 00:06:57.464
I can't deny that I grieved for his grief, whatever that was, and would have given much to assuage it.

59
00:06:57.465 --> 00:07:04.989
He had now been at Thornfield for eight weeks, a very long time for him, and I would miss him if he left.

60
00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:11.313
At last I fell asleep, but I was startled awake by the sound of something brushing against my door.

61
00:07:11.314 --> 00:07:13.978
"Who's there?" I called out.

62
00:07:13.979 --> 00:07:19.275
No one answered, and I felt cold with fear.
